  {"Beyond Safe Migrations", 20, ["Joe Harrow"]},
  {"Introducing Elixir Observer", 20, ["Juan Azambuja"]},
  {"How LiveDebugger makes your life easier", 20, ["Krzysztof Nalepa"]},
  {"Telemetry-Driven Development", 20, ["Noah Betzen"]},
  {"Stranger Domains", 20, ["Nathan Hessler"]},
  {"Building Websites with Tableau", 20, ["Mitchell Hanberg"]},
  {"Writing Your Own Req Plugin", 20, ["Jacob Swanner"]},
  {"UXIDs in Elixir/Ecto", 20, ["Adam Kirk"]},
  {"Full-Stack AI with Elixir", 40, ["George Guimarães"]},
  {"Hey Ash, Let's Chat!", 40, ["Jinkyou Son"]},
  {"The Alchemy of Security", 40, ["Matt Ludwigs"]},
  {"Cat and Mouse: Adversarial Web Scraping", 40, ["Tyler Young"]},
  {"From Freakout to Fix", 40, ["Jonatan Männchen"]},
  {"Failing to Introduce Elixir", 40, ["John Darrington"]},
  {"Elixir is All You Need", 20, ["Christian Koch", "Jeremy Searls"]},
  {"Embracing the Monolith", 20, ["Igor Spiridonov"]},
  {"Building an Exception Tracker", 20, ["Gonzalo Rodriguez"]},
  {"Tips & Tricks for Building an App With LiveView", 40, ["Michael St Clair"]},
  {"Vibe Coding with Elixir", 40, ["Micah Woods"]},
  {"@auth and Beyond", 40, ["Ivy Markwell"]},
  {"Laying the Groundwork", 20, ["Kate Rezentes"]},
  {"Handoff - distributed DAGs for Nx", 20, ["Paulo Valente"]},
  {"AI Agents - Elixir is all you need", 20, ["Daniel Kentfield"]},
  {"How to be an Elixir champion", 20, ["Dan Janowski"]},
  {"IEx's Hidden Superpowers", 20, ["Brett Beatty"]},
  {"Are you mocking me?", 20, ["German Velasco"]},
  {"Phoenix and Ecto Taught Me Backend Engineering", 20, ["Doug VonMoser"]},
  {"Let's talk a bit about bits and bytes", 20, ["Geoffrey Lessel"]},
  {"From Bulbasaur to Venusaur", 20, ["Anna Sherman"]}
]

sessions =
  Enum.map(talks, fn {title, minutes, presenters} ->
    {:ok, presenting} = Agenda.Resource.fetch_all(speakers, presenters)

    Agenda.session(title, duration: Tempo.Duration.new!(minute: minutes))
    |> Agenda.Session.needs(:room, seats: at_least(250))
    |> Agenda.Session.roster(:speaker, presenting)
  end)

length(sessions)
```

## Keynotes are pins

A keynote is published months ahead. It is not a decision left to the scheduler — it is a fixed point the scheduler has to work around.

A pin is not a special kind of value. It is an ordinary arrangement, so you build one by planning the session against the slot it was announced for and keeping the answer.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
keynotes = [
  {"Keynote: Open Source Resilience", 60, ~o"2025-08-28T09:15/T10:15",
   ["Allison Randal"]},
  {"Elixir Update & Q&A", 40, ~o"2025-08-28T16:05/T16:45", []},
  {"Keynote: Elixir's AI Future", 60, ~o"2025-08-29T09:10/T10:10", []},
  {"Keynote: A New Case for Elixir", 60, ~o"2025-08-29T16:20/T17:20", []}
]

plenaries =
  Enum.map(keynotes, fn {title, minutes, slot, presenters} ->
    duration =
      Tempo.Duration.new!(minute: minutes)

    session =
      Agenda.session(title, duration: duration, window: slot)
      |> Agenda.Session.roster(:room, [peninsula])

    {:ok, presenting} =
      Agenda.Resource.fetch_all(speakers, presenters)

    Agenda.Session.roster(session, :speaker, presenting)
  end)

pinned =
  Enum.map(plenaries, fn session ->
    {:ok, [arrangement | _rest]} = Agenda.plan(session, rooms ++ speakers)
    arrangement
  end)

length(pinned)
```

## One session, or all of them

Both verbs have now appeared — `plan/3` produced the keynote pins, and `arrange/3` is about to lay out the whole programme. They answer different questions, which is worth a moment because their output can look identical.

Three one-hour sessions and one room open nine to twelve. `plan/3` returns three placements for **one** session and you pick one of them. `arrange/3` returns three placements for **three** sessions and you take them all. Same three lines, opposite meanings.

| | `plan/3` | `arrange/3` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Takes | one session | a programme of many |
| Returns | the ways it could be held, ranked | one placement each, all compatible |
| Is | an enumeration | a search |
| When it cannot | `{:error, reason}` | also `{:partial, layout}` |

Arranging is built on planning: it asks `plan/3` for each session's candidates, then searches the combinations. Planning never asks whether two sessions can coexist, and that question is the whole of arranging — a room taken by the keynote is gone for the workshop.

## Laying it out

Forty-two talks and four plenaries, against three rooms and forty-five speakers.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
programme =
  Agenda.programme("ElixirConf US 2025")
  |> Agenda.Programme.across(~o"2025-08-28/2025-08-30")
  |> Agenda.Programme.add_sessions(sessions ++ plenaries)

pool = rooms ++ speakers

{microseconds, {:ok, arrangements}} =
  :timer.tc(fn -> Agenda.arrange(programme, pool, pinned: pinned) end)

%{sessions: length(arrangements), milliseconds: div(microseconds, 1000)}
```

## Thursday morning

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
arrangements
|> Enum.filter(&Tempo.within?(&1.interval, ~o"2025-08-28"))
|> Enum.sort(Arrangement)
|> Enum.take(7)
|> Enum.map(fn a ->
  %{
    "Time" => Tempo.to_string(a.interval.from, format: :hm),
    "Room" => Arrangement.resource(a, :room).name,
    "Talk" => a.session
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: ["Time", "Room", "Talk"], name: "Thursday morning")
```

## The constraint held

There is no rule saying Allison Randal cannot be on a panel during her own keynote. It follows from her being a resource with a concurrency of one — and it would have followed identically for a room, a projector or a customer site.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
for who <- ["Allison Randal", "Anna Sherman"],
    a <- Enum.sort(arrangements, Arrangement),
    Enum.any?(Arrangement.resources(a), &(&1.name == who)) do
  %{
    "Speaker" => who,
    "When" => Tempo.to_string(a.interval.from),
    "Room" => Arrangement.resource(a, :room).name,
    "Session" => a.session
  }
end
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: ["Speaker", "When", "Room", "Session"], name: "Two sessions each")
```

## When it cannot hold

Force the panel into the keynote slot and the programme stops being satisfiable.

*Everyone free* is the operative phrase. Both other rooms are empty at 09:15 — the session still cannot be held, because one of the four people it names is on stage next door.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
{:ok, panel} =
  Agenda.Resource.fetch_all(speakers, [
    "Savannah Manning",
    "Anna Sherman",
    "Allison Randal",
    "Lorena Mireles"
  ])

clash =
  Agenda.session("Building Careers, Balancing Life",
    duration: ~o"PT40M",
    window: ~o"2025-08-28T09:15/T09:55"
  )
  |> Agenda.Session.needs(:room, seats: at_least(250))
  |> Agenda.Session.roster(:speaker, panel)

swapped =
  (sessions ++ plenaries)
  |> Enum.reject(&(&1.name == "Building Careers, Balancing Life"))
  |> Kernel.++([clash])

forced =
  Agenda.programme("ElixirConf US 2025")
  |> Agenda.Programme.across(~o"2025-08-28/2025-08-30")
  |> Agenda.Programme.add_sessions(swapped)

case Agenda.arrange(forced, pool, pinned: pinned) do
  {:error, reason} -> Agenda.explain(reason)
  {:ok, _arrangements} -> "unexpectedly satisfiable"
end
```

## Failures are sentences

This is the feature that saves the most time in practice, and it is a design decision rather than a nicety: eligibility and its explanation are computed *together*, so the reason can never drift out of step with the answer.

* Not `false`, not "no availability" — the resource, and what was wrong with it
* Every failing reason, not just the first one found
* The same call answers the positive case

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
asked = [
  {"a 700-seat room with a projector", canaveral,
   Agenda.needs(:room, seats: at_least(700), projector: true)},
  {"a room seating 250", hd(speakers), Agenda.needs(:room, seats: at_least(250))},
  {"a room seating 250", canaveral, Agenda.needs(:room, seats: at_least(250))}
]

asked
|> Enum.map(fn {description, resource, requirement} ->
  %{
    "Asked for" => description,
    "Offered" => resource.name,
    "Answer" => Agenda.explain(requirement, resource)
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(keys: ["Asked for", "Offered", "Answer"], name: "Why not")
```

## Which one moves

`conflict/3` goes further than a reason. When a programme will not hold, it returns the *minimal* set of sessions in tension — remove any one member and the rest fit.

Three forty-minute talks, and Canaveral open for the eighty minutes after lunch. Any two of them fit; all three do not, and no single talk is at fault.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
{:ok, afternoon} =
  Agenda.resource("Canaveral", seats: 250) |> Agenda.open(~o"2025-08-28T13:45/T15:05")

three = sessions |> Enum.filter(&(&1.duration == ~o"PT40M")) |> Enum.take(3)

one_block =
  Agenda.programme("After lunch")
  |> Agenda.Programme.across(~o"2025-08-28")
  |> Agenda.Programme.add_sessions(three)

{:ok, in_tension} = Agenda.conflict(one_block, [afternoon] ++ speakers)
in_tension
```

## Changeover

Agenda placed talks back to back. The real conference left ten minutes between them — one speaker unplugging while the next sets up — and Agenda did not invent that, because nothing asked for it.

Declared on the room, it holds between every pair the search places. These are four of the conference's own twenty-minute talks, in Peninsula 4-7's Thursday block.

<!-- slide: code -->

```elixir
{:ok, hall} =
  Agenda.resource("Peninsula 4-7", seats: 250, buffer_after: ~o"PT10M")
  |> Agenda.open(~o"2025-08-28T10:45/T12:45")

turnaround =
  Agenda.programme("Turnaround")
  |> Agenda.Programme.across(~o"2025-08-28")
  |> Agenda.Programme.add_sessions(sessions |> Enum.filter(&(&1.duration == ~o"PT20M")) |> Enum.take(4))

{:ok, spaced} = Agenda.arrange(turnaround, [hall] ++ speakers)

spaced
|> Enum.sort(Arrangement)
|> Enum.map(fn a ->
  %{
    "Starts" => Tempo.to_string(a.interval.from, format: :hm),
    "Ends" => Tempo.to_string(a.interval.to, format: :hm),
    "Talk" => a.session
  }
end)
|> Kino.DataTable.new(
  keys: ["Starts", "Ends", "Talk"],
  name: "Ten minutes to reset between each"
)
```

## Model the gap, or carve it out

Turnaround is real time the room is unavailable, so it holds against a booking that already exists *and* against another talk in the same programme.

* `buffer_after` shifts the candidate starts too — 9:00, 9:50, 10:40, 11:30
* The published windows already spend those minutes: 40 + 10, 40 + 10, 20 fills 10:45–12:45 exactly
* Declaring it as well asks for the same ten minutes twice
* Model the changeover **or** carve it out of the hours — never both

## Scale

Shape matters more than size. 1,200 sessions across twenty days take about four times as long as 200 competing for a single day — six times the work for four times the time, because sessions that cannot interact are solved separately and concurrently.

| Programme | Time |
| --- | --- |
| ElixirConf: 46 sessions, 3 rooms, 2 days | ~175 ms |
| 240 sessions over 6 days | ~70 ms |
| 1,200 sessions over 20 days | ~1 s |
| 200 sessions competing for **one** day | ~240 ms |

## Two days is not two problems

I assumed a two-day conference would split cleanly down the middle. It does not, and the reason is a person.

* Anna Sherman speaks on Thursday **and** Friday
* One shared speaker links both days into a single component
* One shared room, or one precedence, would do the same
* Worth knowing before assuming a long conference decomposes

## Where this stops

Agenda is a scheduler. Timetabling — a school or university week — is a different problem that shares the vocabulary.

* A timetable is a repeating *grid* of periods, not dated intervals
* A lesson splits: "five periods a week, at most two a day, one double"
* Feasibility is easy there; minimising student gaps is the whole job
* Every teacher and cohort recurs daily, so nothing decomposes
* `Timetable` could be a sibling library, its definitely no in scope for this one

